{
  "id": "ch-series",
  "chapterNumber": "10",
  "title": {
    "en": "Sequences &amp; Series",
    "ar": null
  },
  "content": {
    "blocks": [
      {
        "kind": "text",
        "body": {
          "en": "A series adds infinitely many terms. Remarkably, many familiar functions can be written as “infinite polynomials” — and a short stretch of that polynomial already approximates the function beautifully near a point.",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "callout",
        "style": "def",
        "label": {
          "en": "● Definition 10.1 — Series &amp; convergence",
          "ar": null
        },
        "body": {
          "en": "An infinite series \\( \\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} a_n \\) converges if its partial sums \\( S_N=\\sum_{n=1}^{N} a_n \\) approach a finite limit as \\( N\\to\\infty \\); otherwise it diverges.",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "callout",
        "style": "thm",
        "label": {
          "en": "▲ Theorem 10.2 — Geometric series",
          "ar": null
        },
        "body": {
          "en": "<p>For \\( |r|&lt;1 \\), the geometric series converges:</p>\\[ \\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty} a\\,r^{n}=\\frac{a}{1-r}. \\]<p style=\"margin-bottom:0\">If \\( |r|\\geq 1 \\) it diverges. The \\( p \\)-series \\( \\sum 1/n^{p} \\) converges exactly when \\( p&gt;1 \\).</p>",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "callout",
        "style": "thm",
        "label": {
          "en": "▲ Theorem 10.3 — Taylor &amp; Maclaurin series",
          "ar": null
        },
        "body": {
          "en": "<p>Near \\( x=0 \\), a smooth function equals its Maclaurin series \\( \\displaystyle f(x)=\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}\\frac{f^{(n)}(0)}{n!}x^{n} \\). Three to know:</p>\\[ e^{x}=\\sum \\frac{x^{n}}{n!},\\quad \\sin x=\\sum \\frac{(-1)^{n}x^{2n+1}}{(2n+1)!},\\quad \\cos x=\\sum \\frac{(-1)^{n}x^{2n}}{(2n)!}. \\]",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "text",
        "body": {
          "en": "The explorer builds the Maclaurin polynomial for \\( \\sin x \\) one term at a time. With a single term it is just the line \\( y=x \\); add more and the amber curve wraps itself around \\( \\sin x \\) across an ever-wider interval.",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "explorer",
        "title": {
          "en": "⊙ Explorer · Taylor polynomials of sin x",
          "ar": null
        },
        "canvasId": "taylorCanvas",
